ame":"grok","highlighted_label":{"description":"xAI","badge":{"url":"https:\\/\\/pbs.twimg.com\\/profile_images\\/1769430779845611520\\/lIgjSJGU_bigger.jpg"},"url":{"urlType":"DeepLink","url":"https:\\/\\/twitter.com\\/xai"},"userLabelType":"BusinessLabel","userLabelDisplayType":"Badge"},"verified":false,"is_blue_verified":true,"verified_type":"Business"},"id":"0o7M71JnIvqYSeyX7tI54bDbx","conversation_preview":[{"message":"What ever happened to the Houston texas teacher certification fraud arrests?  How many teachers were certified by this scheme are currently working as Teachers in Texas or elsewhere?","sender":"USER","grokMode":"NORMAL","mediaUrls":[]},{"message":"The Houston, Texas teacher certification fraud arrests involved a significant cheating scandal where multiple individuals were charged for orchestrating a scheme that led to the fraudulent certification of hundreds of teachers. Here\'s an update based on the available information:\\nArrests and Charges: Five individuals were arrested and charged in connection with this scheme, including three Houston Independent School District (HISD) employees. The charges included engaging in organized criminal activity, with the scheme allegedly netting over $1 million. The arrests were announced in late October 2024.\\nScope of the Fraud: \\n  - The scheme is believed to have led to the certification of at least 200 teachers who were not qualified. \\n  - It\'s reported that more than 400 fraudulent exams were taken, with over 200 teachers falsely certified. \\n  - These teachers were dispersed across Texas schools, not just limited to the Houston area.\\nCurrent Status of Fraudulently Certified Teachers:\\n  - By early December 2024, the Texas Education Agency (TEA) had identified 102 teachers under investigation for potentially obtaining fraudulent certifications through this scheme. \\n  - Of these, at least 38 teachers were from Houston-area school districts, with others spread across North and Southeast Texas, including significant numbers in the Dallas-Fort Worth area.\\n  - The exact number of these teachers still actively teaching in Texas or elsewhere after the scandal broke isn\'t explicitly stated, but actions were taken:\\n    - HISD confirmed that any teacher found to have fraudulently obtained their certification would be terminated. \\n    - The TEA was reviewing and investigating these teachers, with the potential for their certifications to be revoked, meaning they would no longer be able to teach legally in Texas or potentially elsewhere if other states recognize Texas\'s certification actions.\\nLegal and Educational Consequences:\\n  - The defendants faced felony charges with potential sentences ranging from two years to life in prison.\\n  - School districts impacted by this scheme, like HISD, were cooperating with the TEA and law enforcement to address the fallout, ensuring fraudulent certifications were identified and dealt with.\\nGiven this information, while the exact number of teachers from this scheme still actively teaching isn\'t specified, it\'s clear that:\\nEfforts are being made to remove fraudulently certified teachers from classrooms. \\nThe investigation continues, and more teachers could be added to the list of those under scrutiny.\\nThese updates are based on information from late 2024, with the expectation that further actions might have been taken since then to address the situation comprehensively.","sender":"AGENT","grokMode":"NORMAL","mediaUrls":[]}],"destination":"destination_1","allowed_actions":[],"metadata":{"text":"Answer by Grok, expand to see full conversation"}}}},"destination_objects":{"destination_1":{"type":"browser","data":{"url_data":{"url":"https:\\/\\/x.com\\/i\\/grok\\/share\\/0o7M71JnIvqYSeyX7tI54bDbx","vanity":"Grok"}}}},"components":["details_1"]}', 'type': 'STRING'}}, {'key': 'card_url', 'value': {'scribe_key': 'card_url', 'string_value': 'https://t.co/yZ24ukqLcF', 'type': 'STRING'}}], 'card_platform': {'platform'